From 3174625380254ace5cec72bc2a730f85fa3763a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rafe Walker <45343084+rafe-walker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 12:10:18 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 04/19] feat(core): add resources_patch with an optional
 resourceVersion precondition (C2)

Every edit went through resources_create_or_update: read the object,
modify it, re-apply it whole. On the hand pass that was 10 copy reads
and 21,141 tokens of reads, resends and echoes, and it could not remove
a field another manager owns (server-side apply only removes fields its
own manager set), so a finalizer could not be removed and job 10 could
not finish. Upstream #1352 (open) proposes the same tool; this follows
its shape: resources_patch(apiVersion, kind, name, namespace,
patchType: strategic | merge | json, patch).

Additions to #1352's proposal:
- `resourceVersion` (optional): a version-checked partial update. For
  merge and strategic patches it is put in metadata.resourceVersion,
  for JSON patches as a leading `test` operation; the API refuses the
  patch with a conflict if the object changed since that version was
  read, and the error says to read it again.
- the patch may be JSON or YAML;
- the result is a receipt (identity and new resourceVersion), or the
  whole object with `output: full`.

Annotations: destructive (a patch can remove fields), not idempotent (a
JSON patch that adds to a list is not), open world. Like every non-read
tool it is hidden under read_only. RBAC metadata: patch on the target.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
